  "problem": "The number of dogs and cats in shelter A is shown in the following table:\n\n| Dogs | Cats |\n| --- | --- |\n| $51$ | $32$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn shelter B there are approximately $20$ dogs. The ratio of the number of dogs to the number of cats in both shelters is the same. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of cats in shelter B.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's round the number of dogs and cats in shelter A:\n\n- Dogs: $51⟶50$\n\n- Cats: $32⟶30$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of dogs to the number of cats (expressed as a fraction) is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{50}{30}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of dogs and the denominator represents the number of cats.\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$ gives an equivalent fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{50÷10}{30÷10}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 4}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{20}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $12$ cats in shelter B.",
